In the valley where the rivers whispered secrets, there stood a tree older than memory, its roots tangled with the songs of the earth. The locals called it the Listening Tree, for it did not speak aloud, but listened—to every breath, every heartbeat, every dream that drifted toward its silver leaves at night.

A girl named Lina tended the tree as if it were a patient with a fever of stories. Each day she brought a bowl of water from the spring and poured it at the tree’s base, listening for a sigh in the bark. One evening, a storm gathered, and the wind spoke in a language only the Listening Tree could comprehend. Thunder rolled like drums, and a single gust set loose a seed from the tree’s crown, falling into Lina’s palm.

The seed hummed with a quiet heat, and Lina realized it wasn’t a seed at all but a memory from the earth itself. When planted, it unfurled into a new branch that bore not leaves, but tiny lanterns that glowed with the faces of people who had come before. Each lantern told a story—the tale of a hunter who found his way by following a star, the tale of a child who learned to hear the rain, the tale of an old woman who learned to forgive the storm inside her.

Lina learned to listen not only with her ears but with the space between words. The lanterns taught her that stories live where attention rests, and attention is a gift you give to the world. The Listening Tree, grateful, grew quieter, and the valley began to hum with a gentler, wiser silence.
